Here's the Java code to implement this algorithm:
```java
public class Solution {
    public int longestCommonSubsequence(String s1, String s2) {
        int m = s1.length();
        int n = s2.length();

        // Create a two-dimensional array to store the length of the common subsequence
        int[][] dp = new int[m+1][n+1];

        // Initialize the first row and column with 0s
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            dp[i][0] = 0;
        }
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            dp[0][j] = 0;
        }

        // Fill in the rest of the array using the recurrence relation
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (s1.charAt(i-1) == s2.charAt(j-1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
                }
            }
        }

        // Return the length of the longest common subsequence
        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
```
In this code, we first initialize the two-dimensional array `dp` with all zeros. Then, we fill in the rest of the array using the recurrence relation:

* If the current characters in `s1` and `s2` are the same, we include the length of the common subsequence from the previous row and column.
* If the characters are different, we choose the longer subsequence from the previous row or column (whichever is not the current row or column) and add 1 to the length.

Finally, we return the length of the longest common subsequence from the last row and column of the array.

